# async/await, Tokio & Networking

## What async really is

`async fn` compiles to a state machine returning a `Future`. Futures do nothing
until polled by an **executor** — Tokio is the standard one:

```rust
#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    let result = fetch().await;      // yields to the executor while waiting
    println!("{result}");
}

async fn fetch() -> &'static str { "payload" }
```

`.await` is a *suspension point*, not a thread block. Thousands of tasks can
multiplex over few threads.

## Bounded time: tokio::time

```rust
use tokio::time::{timeout, Duration};

match timeout(Duration::from_secs(5), slow_task()).await {
    Ok(value) => println!("got {value}"),
    Err(_) => println!("timed out"),
}
```

This exact pattern guards this app's execution sandbox (5 s hard cap).

## TCP with tokio::net

```rust
use tokio::io::{AsyncReadExt, AsyncWriteExt};
use tokio::net::TcpListener;

let listener = TcpListener::bind("127.0.0.1:8080").await?;
loop {
    let (mut socket, addr) = listener.accept().await?;
    tokio::spawn(async move {
        let mut buf = [0u8; 1024];
        let n = socket.read(&mut buf).await.unwrap_or(0);
        socket.write_all(&buf[..n]).await.ok();   // echo
        let _ = addr;
    });
}
```

## HTTP clients: reqwest

For real HTTP use `reqwest` (async, TLS): `reqwest::get(url).await?.text().await?`.
The offline app never performs network calls at runtime — but the Tauri track's
dashboard project teaches the client side.

> ⚠️ The sandbox in *this* app runs plain `rustc`, so external crates like tokio
> aren't importable in lesson code. Exercises here stay std-only.

